{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-11_12-49-23-hashicorp_raft-real-run; grep -n \"''^func '\"\\\\|\"'^// \" agent-source/api.go | sed -n '\"'1,200p'\"","aggregated_output":"1:// Copyright (c) HashiCorp, Inc.\n2:// SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0\n78:// Raft implements a Raft node.\n227:// BootstrapCluster initializes a server's storage with the given cluster\n228:// configuration. This should only be called at the beginning of time for the\n229:// cluster with an identical configuration listing all Voter servers. There is\n230:// no need to bootstrap Nonvoter and Staging servers.\n232:// A cluster can only be bootstrapped once from a single participating Voter\n233:// server. Any further attempts to bootstrap will return an error that can be\n234:// safely ignored.\n236:// One approach is to bootstrap a single server with a configuration\n237:// listing just itself as a Voter, then invoke AddVoter() on it to add other\n238:// servers to the cluster.\n239:func BootstrapCluster(conf *Config, logs LogStore, stable StableStore,\n285:// RecoverCluster is used to manually force a new configuration in order to\n286:// recover from a loss of quorum where the current configuration cannot be\n287:// restored, such as when several servers die at the same time. This works by\n288:// reading all the current state for this server, creating a snapshot with the\n289:// supplied configuration, and then truncating the Raft log. This is the only\n290:// safe way to force a given configuration without actually altering the log to\n291:// insert any new entries, which could cause conflicts with other servers with\n292:// different state.\n294:// WARNING! This operation implicitly commits all entries in the Raft log, so\n295:// in general this is an extremely unsafe operation. If you've lost your other\n296:// servers and are performing a manual recovery, then you've also lost the\n297:// commit information, so this is likely the best you can do, but you should be\n298:// aware that calling this can cause Raft log entries that were in the process\n299:// of being replicated but not yet be committed to be committed.\n301:// Note the FSM passed here is used for the snapshot operations and will be\n302:// left in a state that should not be used by the application. Be sure to\n303:// discard this FSM and any associated state and provide a fresh one when\n304:// calling NewRaft later.\n306:// A typical way to recover the cluster is to shut down all servers and then\n307:// run RecoverCluster on every server using an identical configuration. When\n308:// the cluster is then restarted, and election should occur and then Raft will\n309:// resume normal operation. If it's desired to make a particular server the\n310:// leader, this can be used to inject a new configuration with that server as\n311:// the sole voter, and then join up other new clean-state peer servers using\n312:// the usual APIs in order to bring the cluster back into a known state.\n313:func RecoverCluster(conf *Config, fsm FSM, logs LogStore, stable StableStore,\n442:// GetConfiguration returns the persisted configuration of the Raft cluster\n443:// without starting a Raft instance or connecting to the cluster. This function\n444:// has identical behavior to Raft.GetConfiguration.\n445:func GetConfiguration(conf *Config, fsm FSM, logs LogStore, stable StableStore,\n460:// HasExistingState returns true if the server has any existing state (logs,\n461:// knowledge of a current term, or any snapshots).\n462:func HasExistingState(logs LogStore, stable StableStore, snaps SnapshotStore) (bool, error) {\n496:// NewRaft is used to construct a new Raft node. It takes a configuration, as well\n497:// as implementations of various interfaces that are required. If we have any\n498:// old state, such as snapshots, logs, peers, etc, all those will be restored\n499:// when creating the Raft node.\n500:func NewRaft(conf *Config, fsm FSM, logs LogStore, stable StableStore, snaps SnapshotStore, trans Transport) (*Raft, error) {\n628:// restoreSnapshot attempts to restore the latest snapshots, and fails if none\n629:// of them can be restored. This is called at initialization time, and is\n630:// completely unsafe to call at any other time.\n631:func (r *Raft) restoreSnapshot() error {\n677:func (r *Raft) tryRestoreSingleSnapshot(snapshot *SnapshotMeta) bool {\n709:func (r *Raft) config() Config {\n713:// ReloadConfig updates the configuration of a running raft node. If the new\n714:// configuration is invalid an error is returned and no changes made to the\n715:// instance. All fields will be copied from rc into the new configuration, even\n716:// if they are zero valued.\n717:func (r *Raft) ReloadConfig(rc ReloadableConfig) error {\n743:// ReloadableConfig returns the current state of the reloadable fields in Raft's\n744:// configuration. This is useful for programs to discover the current state for\n745:// reporting to users or tests. It is safe to call from any goroutine. It is\n746:// intended for reporting and testing purposes primarily; external\n747:// synchronization would be required to safely use this in a read-modify-write\n748:// pattern for reloadable configuration options.\n749:func (r *Raft) ReloadableConfig() ReloadableConfig {\n756:// BootstrapCluster is equivalent to non-member BootstrapCluster but can be\n757:// called on an un-bootstrapped Raft instance after it has been created. This\n758:// should only be called at the beginning of time for the cluster with an\n759:// identical configuration listing all Voter servers. There is no need to\n760:// bootstrap Nonvoter and Staging servers.\n762:// A cluster can only be bootstrapped once from a single participating Voter\n763:// server. Any further attempts to bootstrap will return an error that can be\n764:// safely ignored.\n766:// One sane approach is to bootstrap a single server with a configuration\n767:// listing just itself as a Voter, then invoke AddVoter() on it to add other\n768:// servers to the cluster.\n769:func (r *Raft) BootstrapCluster(configuration Configuration) Future {\n781:// Leader is used to return the current leader of the cluster.\n782:// Deprecated: use LeaderWithID instead\n783:// It may return empty string if there is no current leader\n784:// or the leader is unknown.\n785:// Deprecated: use LeaderWithID instead.\n786:func (r *Raft) Leader() ServerAddress {\n793:// LeaderWithID is used to return the current leader address and ID of the cluster.\n794:// It may return empty strings if there is no current leader\n795:// or the leader is unknown.\n796:func (r *Raft) LeaderWithID() (ServerAddress, ServerID) {\n804:// Apply is used to apply a command to the FSM in a highly consistent\n805:// manner. This returns a future that can be used to wait on the application.\n806:// An optional timeout can be provided to limit the amount of time we wait\n807:// for the command to be started. This must be run on the leader or it\n808:// will fail.\n810:// If the node discovers it is no longer the leader while applying the command,\n811:// it will return ErrLeadershipLost. There is no way to guarantee whether the\n812:// write succeeded or failed in this case. For example, if the leader is\n813:// partitioned it can't know if a quorum of followers wrote the log to disk. If\n814:// at least one did, it may survive into the next leader's term.\n816:// If a user snapshot is restored while the command is in-flight, an\n817:// ErrAbortedByRestore is returned. In this case the write effectively failed\n818:// since its effects will not be present in the FSM after the restore.\n819:func (r *Raft) Apply(cmd []byte, timeout time.Duration) ApplyFuture {\n823:// ApplyLog performs Apply but takes in a Log directly. The only values\n824:// currently taken from the submitted Log are Data and Extensions. See\n825:// Apply for details on error cases.\n826:func (r *Raft) ApplyLog(log Log, timeout time.Duration) ApplyFuture {\n854:// Barrier is used to issue a command that blocks until all preceding\n855:// operations have been applied to the FSM. It can be used to ensure the\n856:// FSM reflects all queued writes. An optional timeout can be provided to\n857:// limit the amount of time we wait for the command to be started. This\n858:// must be run on the leader, or it will fail.\n859:func (r *Raft) Barrier(timeout time.Duration) Future {\n880:// VerifyLeader is used to ensure this peer is still the leader. It may be used\n881:// to prevent returning stale data from the FSM after the peer has lost\n882:// leadership.\n883:func (r *Raft) VerifyLeader() Future {\n895:// GetConfiguration returns the latest configuration. This may not yet be\n896:// committed. The main loop can access this directly.\n897:func (r *Raft) GetConfiguration() ConfigurationFuture {\n905:// AddPeer to the cluster configuration. Must be run on the leader, or it will fail.\n907:// Deprecated: Use AddVoter/AddNonvoter instead.\n908:func (r *Raft) AddPeer(peer ServerAddress) Future {\n921:// RemovePeer from the cluster configuration. If the current leader is being\n922:// removed, it will cause a new election to occur. Must be run on the leader,\n923:// or it will fail.\n925:// Deprecated: Use RemoveServer instead.\n926:func (r *Raft) RemovePeer(peer ServerAddress) Future {\n938:// AddVoter will add the given server to the cluster as a staging server. If the\n939:// server is already in the cluster as a voter, this updates the server's address.\n940:// This must be run on the leader or it will fail. The leader will promote the\n941:// staging server to a voter once that server is ready. If nonzero, prevIndex is\n942:// the index of the only configuration upon which this change may be applied; if\n943:// another configuration entry has been added in the meantime, this request will\n944:// fail. If nonzero, timeout is how long this server should wait before the\n945:// configuration change log entry is appended.\n946:func (r *Raft) AddVoter(id ServerID, address ServerAddress, prevIndex uint64, timeout time.Duration) IndexFuture {\n959:// AddNonvoter will add the given server to the cluster but won't assign it a\n960:// vote. The server will receive log entries, but it won't participate in\n961:// elections or log entry commitment. If the server is already in the cluster,\n962:// this updates the server's address. This must be run on the leader or it will\n963:// fail. For prevIndex and timeout, see AddVoter.\n964:func (r *Raft) AddNonvoter(id ServerID, address ServerAddress, prevIndex uint64, timeout time.Duration) IndexFuture {\n977:// RemoveServer will remove the given server from the cluster. If the current\n978:// leader is being removed, it will cause a new election to occur. This must be\n979:// run on the leader or it will fail. For prevIndex and timeout, see AddVoter.\n980:func (r *Raft) RemoveServer(id ServerID, prevIndex uint64, timeout time.Duration) IndexFuture {\n992:// DemoteVoter will take away a server's vote, if it has one. If present, the\n993:// server will continue to receive log entries, but it won't participate in\n994:// elections or log entry commitment. If the server is not in the cluster, this\n995:// does nothing. This must be run on the leader or it will fail. For prevIndex\n996:// and timeout, see AddVoter.\n997:func (r *Raft) DemoteVoter(id ServerID, prevIndex uint64, timeout time.Duration) IndexFuture {\n1009:// Shutdown is used to stop the Raft background routines.\n1010:// This is not a graceful operation. Provides a future that\n1011:// can be used to block until all background routines have exited.\n1012:func (r *Raft) Shutdown() Future {\n1027:// Snapshot is used to manually force Raft to take a snapshot. Returns a future\n1028:// that can be used to block until complete, and that contains a function that\n1029:// can be used to open the snapshot.\n1030:func (r *Raft) Snapshot() SnapshotFuture {\n1042:// Restore is used to manually force Raft to consume an external snapshot, such\n1043:// as if restoring from a backup. We will use the current Raft configuration,\n1044:// not the one from the snapshot, so that we can restore into a new cluster. We\n1045:// will also use the max of the index of the snapshot, or the current index,\n1046:// and then add 1 to that, so we force a new state with a hole in the Raft log,\n1047:// so that the snapshot will be sent to followers and used for any new joiners.\n1048:// This can only be run on the leader, and blocks until the restore is complete\n1049:// or an error occurs.\n1051:// WARNING! This operation has the leader take on the state of the snapshot and\n1052:// then sets itself up so that it replicates that to its followers though the\n1053:// install snapshot process. This involves a potentially dangerous period where\n1054:// the leader commits ahead of its followers, so should only be used for disaster\n1055:// recovery into a fresh cluster, and should not be used in normal operations.\n1056:func (r *Raft) Restore(meta *SnapshotMeta, reader io.Reader, timeout time.Duration) error {\n1101:// State returns the state of this raft peer.\n1102:func (r *Raft) State() RaftState {\n1106:// LeaderCh is used to get a channel which delivers signals on acquiring or\n1107:// losing leadership. It sends true if we become the leader, and false if we\n1108:// lose it.\n1110:// Receivers can expect to receive a notification only if leadership\n1111:// transition has occurred.\n1113:// If receivers aren't ready for the signal, signals may drop and only the\n1114:// latest leadership transition. For example, if a receiver receives subsequent\n1115:// `true` values, they may deduce that leadership was lost and regained while\n1116:// the receiver was processing first leadership transition.\n1117:func (r *Raft) LeaderCh() <-chan bool {\n1121:// String returns a string representation of this Raft node.\n1122:func (r *Raft) String() string {\n1126:// LastContact returns the time of last contact by a leader.\n1127:// This only makes sense if we are currently a follower.\n1128:func (r *Raft) LastContact() time.Time {\n1135:// Stats is used to return a map of various internal stats. This\n1136:// should only be used for informative purposes or debugging.\n1138:// Keys are: \"state\", \"term\", \"last_log_index\", \"last_log_term\",\n1139:// \"commit_index\", \"applied_index\", \"fsm_pending\",\n1140:// \"last_snapshot_index\", \"last_snapshot_term\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
